well the first thing to notice is that  you can be for markets without being  against redistribution i am nicolas be  with reason TV and today we're joined by  eric Agner he's a philosophy professor  at George Mason University and the  author of the 2007 book Hayek and  natural law and more recently he's  written an interesting article in  Politico about why Friedrich Hayek would  be in favor of centralized health care  or nationalized health care Eric thanks  for joining us pleasure you talk a  little bit about this this is of some  months old but you wrote a piece for  Politico which is interesting from a  libertarian point of view because you  actually point out that Friedrich Hayek  one of the great patron saints of  contemporary libertarianism actually was  in favor of things like nationalized  health care he would have been in favor  of mandates I think the first thing to  know about Hayek is that he was actually  for redistribution so this doesn't sound  like the sort of thing somebody like  Hayek might say you might think he had a  stroke who made a mistake at some point  in his writings but this is actually a  theme that goes back his entire career  in 1944 and wrote has served him he said  something about how he'd always been in  favor of a minimum income for all and  the same theme comes back throughout his  life and then in the constitution of  Liberty which in many ways is considered  his magnum opus he actually talks about  the you know welfare payments minimum  wage but also he signals that he'd be in  favor of some kind of universal health  care that relies on mandates talk a  little bit about how does he square that  with the idea of maximum individual  liberty well the first thing to notice  is that you can be for markets without  being against redistribution right so  for Hayek the central argument for  markets the main reason why markets work  so well has to do with a function of the  price system so Hayek emphasize how  surplus forces of supply and demand  operate in such a way that prices  reflect all sorts of information that  any individual member could not have so  and this also carries through in things  like Milton Friedman who also talked  about a guaranteed minimum  at various points and of course in the  50s popularized the idea created the  idea of school vouchers and the idea was  to create a market in education where  there was more choice and if it wasn't  the price system at least there would be  a discovery process by people choosing  among different options that's right the  important thing to know about Hayek is  that you must not interfere with the  price system unless you absolutely have  to but redistribution is consistent with  having a free-market operate and the  price system will function like it's  supposed to what from a kind of Hayekian  point of view what are the limits on  that though because Hayek certainly  recognizes that whenever government got  too large or actually in the road to  serfdom we talked about how some  planning leads to more planning there's  a seems to be a natural tendency for the  government to grow to a point where it  starts choking off market signals or or  the politics become such that people are  no longer free to act that's right and  there's a fairly bright line in Hayek's  work where he says governments are not  allowed to interfere with the price  system unless it's absolutely necessary  once government's start doing that we're  on the road to serfdom that is there's a  real possibility that we'll descend into  someplace we do not want to be but  redistribution is okay and you could  even argue that some redistribution  makes the markets work more efficiently  what how do you mean by that so the idea  is that the price system works so well  because prices contain information  that's distributed across the society  and some people are precluded from  operating in the market because they  don't have any resources the information  that they have access to won't be  reflected in prices and so some degree  of redistribution is unlimited amount of  redistribution might actually make the  prices in work more efficient now to  talk about something like Obamacare I  mean we're less than a year out from its  full implementation in January of 2014  one of the things that's striking about  it though is that it does have an  individual mandate which was very  controversial had to go all the way to  the Supreme Court before being you know  that it is as constitutional there are  all sorts of ways in which the price  system is massively distorted in  Obamacare so for instance they spread  between what insurers can charge people  over 60 versus people under 30 currently  that's capped at about five times  / you know the average difference it's  gonna be capped at three times which  means that younger people are going to  be paying more for insurance older  people will be paying less is that kind  of violence to pricing something that  would invalidate you know health care  reform from a Hayekian perspective that  aspect of the law would be completely  out of the question from a Hayekian  perspective the idea of price floors or  price ceilings or price controls at all  would displace the function of the price  system what is consistent with a Hayek  envision I think is this idea that you  can restrict resources from people who  have more of it to people who have less  of it and that you can mandate people to  purchase health insurance Hayek does not  spend a whole lot of time talking about  this but I think we can fill in the  blanks so he's concerned about the  minimum income for all he's concerned  about everyone having access to basic  social services and he proposes mandates  essentially as a way to get around this  why would he have done that well the  ideal Hayekian system would really  involve cash transfers to the poor and  they have and these specific things that  I mean it wouldn't be food stamps it  would be here's cash buy food with it  buy cigarettes by health care whatever  you want exactly but is that way the  poor would be maximally free to spend  the money they receive this might be  politically unpalatable this might be  part of the reason why I didn't propose  it but the other thing to note about  health care is that most people can  afford it actually buy it as by giving  people money and mandating that they buy  health insurance you're not too far off  the target why are you interested in the  Hayek and how do you see him fitting  into a broader range of political  philosophy that goes beyond libertarians  so I originally came to Hayek as a  philosophy of science I was a philosophy  of science first I was interested in his  work he's on in biology and evolutionary  biology so Hayek has this theory of  group selection as an essential part of  his project I was originally interested  in criticizing that but then as I read  more and more about Hayek I realized  what an interesting and multi-faceted  figure he is and I actually ended up a  lot more interested in his work on  information and knowledge which is what  we've been talking about do you define  yourself as being on the right or the  left or had it how do you characterize  yourself  in an American context I don't really  use those categories I think they're  unhelpful I have very strong libertarian  leanings although I also see a role for  redistribution I see a role for social  services much like Hayek did do you find  Hayek at various points has been the bet  noir of people on the left and at other  times people especially on the left have  reached and said you know this is a guy  who has something really powerful to say  both to you know people on the  progressive left as well as on the  free-market right  do you see Hayek as a useful person to  Rican to kind of pull together a new  consensus that might get us past some of  the sticking points of 20th century  political philosophy absolutely he's so  much more interesting than both his  critics and he's admires sometimes let  on I get the sounds occasionally that no  one reads him very carefully because  there's so much material there and I  think you're you're absolutely right  he's offering a vision of a middle  ground he's allowing classical liberals  to say to their leftist friends that yes  we're also in favor of redistribution  there's a rule for that but there are  certain balance that we've got to stay  inside and I think that might help us  overcome some of the most vicious  divisions of contemporary American  politics well we'll leave it there we've  been talking to Eric Agner of George  Mason University he's a hike scholar his  2007 book is Hayek and natural law for  recent TV I'm Nicholas

